__registerContent("quickrecall/history/advent-europeans", {"notes":{"qr-advent-europeans":{"source":"Modern History","sections":[{"type":"points","title":"🎯 Order of Arrival (Trading Companies)","items":["Portuguese (1498) → Dutch (1602) → English (1600) → Danish (1616) → French (1664)","By date of COMPANY establishment: English EIC (1600), Dutch VOC (1602), Danish (1616), French (1664)","Portuguese were the FIRST to arrive (Vasco da Gama, 1498) and the LAST to leave (Goa, 1961)","Mnemonic for the sequence of who came: 'Trade → Control → Colonies → British Rule'"]},{"type":"table","title":"🏳 The European Companies","headers":["Company","Est.","Key Facts"],"rows":[["Portuguese","1498 (arrival)","Vasco da Gama reached Calicut (20 May 1498); Francisco de Almeida (Blue Water Policy); Alfonso de Albuquerque (real founder of Portuguese power, captured Goa 1510)"],["English (EIC)","1600","Charter by Queen Elizabeth I; Hawkins & Sir Thomas Roe at Jahangir's court; factory at Surat (1613); presidencies: Madras (Fort St. George), Bombay, Calcutta (Fort William)"],["Dutch (VOC)","1602","United East India Company (VOC); mainly spice trade; focused on Indonesia; defeated by the English at the Battle of Bedara (1759)"],["Danish","1616","Small presence; centres at Tranquebar (Tamil Nadu) and Serampore (Bengal); sold settlements to the British (1845)"],["French","1664","Est. by Colbert; HQ Pondicherry; Dupleix (greatest French governor); lost political power after the Third Carnatic War (Battle of Wandiwash 1760; Treaty of Paris 1763)"]]},{"type":"table","title":"📜 Consolidation of British Power — Timeline","headers":["Year","Event"],"rows":[["1600","English East India Company established (charter by Elizabeth I)"],["1757","Battle of Plassey — turning point of British power in Bengal"],["1764","Battle of Buxar — British supremacy established"],["1765","Diwani rights of Bengal, Bihar & Odisha granted (by Shah Alam II)"],["1773","Regulating Act — first step towards British control over the Company"],["1793","Permanent Settlement introduced by Lord Cornwallis"],["1798–1805","Subsidiary Alliance introduced by Lord Wellesley"],["1813","Charter Act — ended the Company's trade monopoly (except tea & trade with China)"],["1833","Charter Act — Company became a purely administrative body"],["1853","Charter Act — end of Company patronage; more administrative reforms"],["1857","Revolt of 1857 — the First War of Independence"],["1858","Government of India Act — end of Company rule; power transferred to the British Crown"]]},{"type":"traps","title":"⚠ Prelims Traps","items":[{"label":"First to arrive vs first company","lines":["Portuguese arrived first (1498) but the English EIC (1600) was established before the Dutch VOC (1602).","Portuguese were first to come and last to leave India (Goa liberated 1961)."]},{"label":"Plassey vs Buxar","lines":["Plassey (1757): turning point — Robert Clive vs Siraj-ud-Daulah.","Buxar (1764): established British supremacy; led to Diwani (1765)."]},{"label":"Alfonso de Albuquerque","lines":["Real founder of Portuguese power in India; captured Goa in 1510.","Vasco da Gama was the first European to reach India by sea (Calicut, 1498)."]}]},{"type":"recall","title":"🔑 Quick Recall","items":["Arrival order: Portuguese(1498) → Dutch(1602) → English(1600 co.) → Danish(1616) → French(1664)","Vasco da Gama reached Calicut, 20 May 1498","English EIC chartered 1600 by Elizabeth I; factory at Surat 1613","Plassey 1757 (turning point); Buxar 1764 (supremacy); Diwani 1765","Regulating Act 1773; Charter Acts 1813/1833/1853","1858: Crown rule begins (end of Company)","Portuguese: first to come, last to leave (Goa, 1961)"]}]}},"quizzes":{"qr-advent-europeans":{"title":"Advent of Europeans & British Consolidation","questions":[{"q":"Who was the first European to reach India by sea, and where did he land in 1498?","options":["Christopher Columbus; Goa","Vasco da Gama; Calicut","Pedro Cabral; Cochin","Alfonso de Albuquerque; Surat"],"correct":1,"explain":"Vasco da Gama reached Calicut on 20 May 1498, becoming the first European to reach India by a sea route."},{"q":"The English East India Company was granted its charter by:","options":["King James I","Queen Elizabeth I","Queen Victoria","King George III"],"correct":1,"explain":"The English East India Company was established in 1600 under a charter granted by Queen Elizabeth I."},{"q":["Arrange the following European companies by their year of establishment:","1. Dutch (VOC)","2. English EIC","3. French","Select the correct order (earliest first):"],"options":["2 → 1 → 3","1 → 2 → 3","3 → 2 → 1","2 → 3 → 1"],"correct":0,"explain":"English EIC (1600) → Dutch VOC (1602) → French (1664)."},{"q":"Which battle is regarded as the 'turning point' that laid the foundation of British power in Bengal?","options":["Battle of Buxar (1764)","Battle of Plassey (1757)","Battle of Wandiwash (1760)","Third Battle of Panipat (1761)"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Battle of Plassey (1757), where Robert Clive defeated Siraj-ud-Daulah, was the turning point of British power."},{"q":"The Diwani rights of Bengal, Bihar and Odisha were granted to the British in 1765 by:","options":["Siraj-ud-Daulah","Mir Jafar","Shah Alam II","Tipu Sultan"],"correct":2,"explain":"After the Battle of Buxar, the Mughal Emperor Shah Alam II granted the Diwani (revenue rights) of Bengal, Bihar and Odisha to the Company in 1765."},{"q":["Consider the following statements:","I. The Portuguese were the first Europeans to arrive in India.","II. The Portuguese were also the last to leave India.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — the Portuguese arrived first (1498) and were the last to leave (Goa was liberated in 1961)."},{"q":"Who is regarded as the real founder of Portuguese power in India, capturing Goa in 1510?","options":["Francisco de Almeida","Alfonso de Albuquerque","Vasco da Gama","Pedro Alvarez Cabral"],"correct":1,"explain":"Alfonso de Albuquerque, who captured Goa in 1510, is considered the real founder of Portuguese power in India."},{"q":"The Battle of Buxar (1764) is significant because it:","options":["Marked the arrival of the Portuguese","Established British supremacy and led to the grant of Diwani","Ended French power in India","Began the Revolt of 1857"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Battle of Buxar (1764) firmly established British supremacy and led to the Diwani grant of 1765."},{"q":["Match the company with its key centre/HQ:","I. French — a) Serampore","II. Danish — b) Pondicherry","Select the correct answer:"],"options":["I-b, II-a","I-a, II-b","Both had HQ at Surat","Neither can be matched"],"correct":0,"explain":"The French HQ was Pondicherry (b); the Danish had centres at Tranquebar and Serampore (a)."},{"q":"The Dutch East India Company (VOC) was primarily interested in:","options":["Christian missionary activity","Spice trade (focused on Indonesia)","Territorial conquest of North India","Cotton textile manufacturing"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Dutch VOC concentrated on the spice trade, with its main focus on the Indonesian archipelago."},{"q":"Which Act of 1858 transferred the governance of India from the East India Company to the British Crown?","options":["The Regulating Act","The Charter Act 1853","The Government of India Act, 1858","The Indian Councils Act 1861"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Government of India Act, 1858 ended Company rule and transferred power to the British Crown after the Revolt of 1857."},{"q":"The Charter Act of 1813 is significant because it:","options":["Ended the Company's trade monopoly (except tea and trade with China)","Established the Supreme Court at Calcutta","Abolished the Company entirely","Introduced the Permanent Settlement"],"correct":0,"explain":"The Charter Act of 1813 ended the Company's trade monopoly, except for the tea trade and trade with China."},{"q":"Sir Thomas Roe visited the court of which Mughal emperor to secure trading rights for the English?","options":["Akbar","Jahangir","Shah Jahan","Aurangzeb"],"correct":1,"explain":"Sir Thomas Roe was an ambassador to the court of Jahangir (1615), following William Hawkins."},{"q":["Consider the following statements:","I. The English established their first factory at Surat.","II. The Charter Act of 1833 made the Company a purely administrative body.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — the English established a factory at Surat (1613), and the Charter Act 1833 turned the Company into an administrative body."},{"q":"The French lost their political ambitions in India following which battle/treaty?","options":["Battle of Plassey / Treaty of Alinagar","Battle of Wandiwash (1760) / Treaty of Paris (1763)","Battle of Buxar (1764)","Battle of Bedara (1759)"],"correct":1,"explain":"The French were defeated at the Battle of Wandiwash (1760) in the Third Carnatic War; the Treaty of Paris (1763) ended their political ambitions."},{"q":"The Regulating Act of 1773 is significant as:","options":["The first step towards British parliamentary control over the Company","The Act that abolished the Company","The Act that granted Diwani","The Act that ended Crown rule"],"correct":0,"explain":"The Regulating Act of 1773 was the first step by the British Parliament to control and regulate the affairs of the East India Company."},{"q":"The Battle of Bedara (1759) was fought between the English and the:","options":["French","Portuguese","Dutch","Danish"],"correct":2,"explain":"At the Battle of Bedara (1759), the English defeated the Dutch, ending Dutch ambitions in India."},{"q":"Which of the following correctly represents the broad pattern of European involvement in India?","options":["British Rule → Colonies → Control → Trade","Trade → Control → Colonies → British Rule","Colonies → Trade → British Rule → Control","Control → Trade → British Rule → Colonies"],"correct":1,"explain":"Europeans came for trade, then established control, then colonies, culminating in British Rule — 'Trade → Control → Colonies → British Rule'."},{"q":"The three English presidency capitals were:","options":["Delhi, Agra and Lahore","Madras (Fort St. George), Bombay and Calcutta (Fort William)","Surat, Goa and Pondicherry","Tranquebar, Serampore and Chinsurah"],"correct":1,"explain":"The English presidencies were centred at Madras (Fort St. George), Bombay, and Calcutta (Fort William)."},{"q":"The Permanent Settlement of 1793 was introduced by:","options":["Lord Wellesley","Lord Cornwallis","Warren Hastings","Lord Dalhousie"],"correct":1,"explain":"Lord Cornwallis introduced the Permanent Settlement (Zamindari system) in Bengal in 1793."}]}}});
